WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:09.540
Trajan: Optimus Princeps

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:22.150
“Felicior Augusto, melior Traiano”.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:26.480
According to 4th century historian Eutropius,
that was the acclamation used to greet new

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.190
Roman emperors in his time.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:33.480
It expressed hope that the ruler will be “more
fortunate than Augustus and better than Trajan!”

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.610
The historian lived 200 years after Trajan,
but the memory of his reign of prosperity

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:41.710
was still very much alive in the hearts and
minds of the Roman people.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:46.780
Trajan was part of the “Five Good Emperors”
- five successive rulers who presided over

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.910
an 85-year period of affluence for the Roman
Empire.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:54.550
Of those emperors, we can safely say that
Trajan was the best of the bunch.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:59.100
He enacted welfare policies for the poor,
restored confiscated properties and undertook

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.289
numerous construction projects.

00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:05.399
Within his borders, he brought stability,
while outwardly, he led military campaigns

00:01:05.399 --> 00:01:09.590
which saw the Roman Empire expand to its greatest
size ever.

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:14.229
He became the paragon that all subsequent
emperors aspired to emulate although none

00:01:14.229 --> 00:01:16.070
of them reached the same heights.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:20.600
To honor his accomplishments, the Roman Senate
bestowed upon Trajan the title of “Optimus

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.460
Princeps” - “the best ruler”.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:31.729
Family and Military Life
Trajan was born Marcus Ulpius Traianus on

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:36.099
September 18, 53 CE, in the city of Italica.

00:01:36.099 --> 00:01:41.060
This was located in Hispania Baetica, a Roman
province which roughly corresponds to the

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.079
modern region of Andalusia in Spain.

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:49.899
This is, in of itself, significant because
Trajan will become the first Roman emperor

00:01:49.899 --> 00:01:51.610
to be born outside of Italy.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:55.780
His father was Marcus Ulpius Traianus the
Elder, a Roman statesman, while his mother

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.579
was Marcia, a noblewoman who was the sister-in-law
of Emperor Titus.

00:01:59.579 --> 00:02:04.829
He also had an older sister named Ulpia Marciana
who remained very close to her brother throughout

00:02:04.829 --> 00:02:05.829
their lives.

00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:09.720
Trajan would often consult her as emperor
when making important decisions.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.640
Sometime during his reign, he bestowed upon
Ulpia the imperial title of Augusta, making

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.819
her the first sister of a Roman ruler to receive
this honor.

00:02:17.819 --> 00:02:23.920
Trajan extended this love and trust to Ulpia’s
daughter, Salonia Matidia, as he himself never

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:24.920
had any children.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:30.180
After Ulpia’s death, Trajan had her deified
and gave the title of Augusta to his niece.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.680
As a young man, Trajan distinguished himself
with his military service.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.390
He quickly rose through the ranks, undoubtedly
helped by his father’s position and influence.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:44.431
Sometime around the year 86 CE, his cousin,
Publius Aelius Hadrianus Afer, died and Trajan

00:02:44.431 --> 00:02:49.010
became one of the guardians for his two children
- his daughter, Aelia Domitia Paulina, and

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:51.810
his son, Publius Aelius Hadrianus the Younger.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:56.609
The son would later succeed Trajan to the
throne of Rome as Emperor Hadrian.

00:02:56.609 --> 00:03:00.120
Also during this period, he met and married
Pompeia Plotina.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.620
Ancient scholars portrayed her as being a
kind and intelligent woman.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:07.560
She took an active role during her husband’s
reign, sponsoring initiatives to help the

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.340
poor and improve education.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:13.709
She outlived Trajan by a few years and, when
she passed on, Hadrian built her a temple

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:15.200
and deified her.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.159
Trajan’s military life is poorly documented.

00:03:17.159 --> 00:03:20.930
We know that he was successful and well-liked
by his fellow soldiers, something that would

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:23.680
prove vital to his ascent to power later on.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.200
He served as a tribune for 10 or so years,
and traveled to Syria when his father became

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.530
governor there.

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:33.920
In 89 CE, Trajan was in command of his own
legion and was sent to either Hispania or

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:37.439
Upper Germania, to put down a coup against
Emperor Domitian.

00:03:37.439 --> 00:03:42.990
This earned him the favor of the ruler who
granted Trajan a consulship in 91.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:49.579
Domitian
In order to get a proper understanding of

00:03:49.579 --> 00:03:54.249
Trajan’s rise to the throne, we need a little
background on the two emperors who came before

00:03:54.249 --> 00:03:56.980
him: the aforementioned Domitian and Nerva.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:01.360
Domitian ended up being the last ruler of
the Flavian Dynasty and ancient sources would

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.889
have us believe that he was a cruel, ruthless,
and egomaniacal tyrant.

00:04:05.889 --> 00:04:10.010
Modern historians tend to disagree, however,
and assert that these characterizations of

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:14.799
the emperor were part of a concentrated effort
to denigrate him after his death.

00:04:14.799 --> 00:04:19.840
It is almost certain that Domitian was an
autocrat who ruled with an iron fist and instituted

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:25.060
a cult of personality around himself, but
his totalitarian tendencies were usually focused

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:28.120
on one particular group: the aristocracy of
Rome.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:32.740
Specifically, he was constantly at odds with
the Senate whose powers the emperor curtailed.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:36.930
His policies and propaganda actually made
the emperor pretty popular with the people

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:37.930
and the army.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:41.740
Domitian was assassinated on September 18,
96 CE.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:46.100
According to Suetonius, he became “an object
of terror and hatred to all” and was the

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:50.590
target of a conspiracy organized by his own
friends and “favorite freedmen”.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:05:01.000
The emperor was slain in his chambers where
he was stabbed seven times.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.990
Nerva was crowned the new ruler of Rome and
he immediately began restoring many of the

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:08.509
liberties and privileges taken away by his
predecessor.

00:05:08.509 --> 00:05:12.930
Nerva was the first of the so-called “Five
Good Emperors” and, as you would expect,

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.210
he did many things to earn this reputation.

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:19.830
He brought people back from exile, put a halt
to treason trials and gave a lot of land to

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:20.830
the poor.

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:25.229
He also instituted numerous policies to save
money such as abolishing many sacrifices and

00:05:25.229 --> 00:05:30.110
spectacles, selling large gold and silver
statues commissioned by Domitian and forbidding

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:33.539
that any such sculptures should be made in
his own honor.

00:05:33.539 --> 00:05:37.860
Although Nerva was popular with the people
and the Senate, he failed to appease the army

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.000
where support for Domitian was still strong.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:45.129
The Praetorian Guard demanded that the conspirators
who assassinated the previous emperor be executed,

00:05:45.129 --> 00:05:46.330
but Nerva refused.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:51.860
A quick side note here to give a little historical
context on how important it was for an emperor

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.750
to have the approval of the Praetorian Guard.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:59.440
Although they began as an elite military unit,
they became the emperor’s personal bodyguards

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:00.790
under Augustus.

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:05.009
From there, they kept growing in size and
influence until they turned into the de facto

00:06:05.009 --> 00:06:06.330
police force of Rome.

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:11.129
There have been quite a few intrigues and
assassinations where the Guard played a vital

00:06:11.129 --> 00:06:12.129
role.

00:06:12.129 --> 00:06:17.930
Maybe we’ll talk about a few of them some
day.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:22.449
On one particularly egregious occasion, the
Praetorian Guard killed the emperor and then,

00:06:22.449 --> 00:06:26.659
effectively, sold the throne of Rome by granting
their support to the highest bidder.

00:06:26.659 --> 00:06:33.330
This happened in 193 CE when they killed Pertinax
and accepted an offer of 25,000 sesterces

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:37.280
each to make Didius Julianus the new Emperor
of Rome.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:42.729
His reign lasted a whopping 66 days before
he was also assassinated.

00:06:42.729 --> 00:06:48.390
Back to Nerva, there was another problem with
his reign - he was old and in poor health.

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:52.860
He was 66 years old when he took the throne
and had no children or close relatives.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:57.470
The discontent of the army plus the lack of
a clear successor pretty much guaranteed a

00:06:57.470 --> 00:06:59.060
civil war upon his death.

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:03.889
In an attempt to pacify the soldiers, Nerva
assigned command of the Praetorian Guard to

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:09.039
Casperius Aelianus who served as praetorian
prefect under Domitian.

00:07:09.039 --> 00:07:14.830
This tactic backfired because Aelianus incited
the men to get justice for their former emperor.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:19.990
In July or August 97 CE, the Praetorian Guard
lay siege to the imperial palace, took the

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:24.020
men responsible for Domitian’s death by
force and executed them.

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:27.750
Nerva was not harmed, but his authority was
in tatters.

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:32.120
Only one thing could help him keep his position
- naming an heir who was popular with the

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:59.189
people, the army, and

00:07:59.189 --> 00:08:10.800
the Senate.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:40.000
That heir was Trajan.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:59.720
Rise to

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:08.780
the Throne
Trajan’s appointment came just in time.

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:12.000
He was named heir in October 97 CE.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:17.350
A few months later, on January 27, Nerva died
of natural causes after 15 months as emperor.

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:22.150
Trajan, who was governor of Germania Inferior
when he heard the news, was in no hurry to

00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:27.270
get to Rome to start his new career, instead
taking a lengthy tour of the empire’s frontiers.

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:31.970
Scholars had suggested he did this to ensure
he had the loyalty of the soldiers across

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:33.080
the Roman domain.

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:37.020
He did see fit to deal with the praetorians
who revolted against Nerva, though.

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:42.080
He sent for Aelianus and the rest of the mutineers
to come to Germany for special employment

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:46.690
and there, according to Cassius Dio, he “put
them out of the way”.

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:51.360
It wasn’t until the summer of 99 CE that
Trajan finally set foot in Rome as emperor.

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:55.940
Dio takes the opportunity here to compliment
Plotina, Trajan’s wife, who, upon entering

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:59.250
the palace for the first time, turned around
to the crowd and said “I enter here such

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:02.470
a woman as I would fain be when I depart”.

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:07.580
And, indeed, according to the historian, she
exhibited behavior beyond reproach during

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:10.050
the entire reign.

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:17.080
The Dacian Wars
As emperor, Trajan first turned his attention

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:22.630
to Dacia, a kingdom that mostly corresponds
to modern-day Romania and Moldova.

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:25.440
Dacia had been a nuisance to Rome, on and
off, for a few centuries.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:30.880
During the mid 1st century BCE, a powerful
Dacian king called Burebista had united all

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:32.840
the various tribes under his rule.

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:37.020
Caesar was planning to lead a campaign there,
but was killed in 44 BCE.

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:41.270
However, Burebista was assassinated that same
year and the kingdom slowly splintered after

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:42.270
his death.

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:46.520
It wasn’t until over a century later that
it reemerged as a menace under King Duras

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:49.530
and attacked the Roman province of Moesia.

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:51.370
Domitian was emperor at the time.

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:55.390
He had to respond to this invasion so he launched
the First Dacian War.

00:10:55.390 --> 00:11:02.120
In 87 CE, a Roman army led by General Cornelius
Fuscus crossed the Danube and suffered a humiliating

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:03.680
loss at Tapae.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:07.890
The next year, a renewed offensive was more
successful, but Domitian still had to accept

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:12.480
peace on favorable terms to the Dacians because
his army was needed someplace else to put

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:13.890
down a rebellion.

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:19.080
The new Dacian King Decebalus might have declared
himself a client of Rome but, in exchange,

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:24.140
he received money and craftsmen which he used
to improve Dacia’s defenses and arm its

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:25.140
soldiers.

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:28.990
For decades, this peace was a bit of an embarrassment
for the mighty Roman Empire, especially since

00:11:28.990 --> 00:11:32.680
the Dacians still liked to carry on border
raids from time to time.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:38.310
Cassius Dio described Decebalus as a “worthy
antagonist of the Romans”, a king who was

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:42.760
“shrewd in his understanding of warfare”,
and a “master in pitched battles”.

00:11:42.760 --> 00:11:48.230
Clearly, Trajan agreed because he saw it necessary
to deal with the threat once and for all and

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:52.290
restore some of the glory lost during Domitian’s
failed campaign.

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:57.390
Trajan got the Senate’s blessing to go to
war and, in 101 CE, the Roman army crossed

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:00.810
the Danube River once again and invaded Dacia.

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:05.440
The Roman Emperor obtained a major victory
over Decebalus at the Second Battle of Tapae.

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:09.880
A few more minor conflicts followed but, in
the end, Trajan decided to sue for peace because

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:13.130
he didn’t want his army to get caught in
Dacia during winter.

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:17.230
This time, though, the terms were considerably
in Rome’s favor.

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:21.180
Among other concessions, the Dacians had to
build a stone bridge over the Danube which

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:25.370
would make it a lot easier for Roman troops
to cross the river should they invade Dacia

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:26.370
again.

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:30.010
This structure, simply known as Trajan’s
Bridge, was considered quite a marvel of its

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:31.010
time.

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:35.110
It was designed by a brilliant Greek architect
called Apollodorus of Damascus who would also

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:38.490
be responsible for other constructions ordered
by Trajan.

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:43.090
The bridge only stayed functional for a few
decades until Emperor Aurelian destroyed it,

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:48.000
but it remained the longest bridge of its
kind for over 1,000 years.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:53.800
There was peace, but Trajan understood that
it was only a moment of respite until the

00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:55.340
Dacians regained their strength.

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:58.110
In 105 CE, the two sides went to war again.

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:02.740
This time, the fighting was more neck and
neck as the Romans struggled to gain a decisive

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:03.740
victory.

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:06.890
In 106, they laid siege to the Dacian capital
of Sarmizegetusa and eventually forced the

00:13:06.890 --> 00:13:10.250
people inside to surrender by destroying their
water supply.

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:13.060
Afterwards, the capital was razed to the ground.

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:17.680
Decebalus committed suicide instead of being
taken prisoner and his head was brought back

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:18.680
to Rome.

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:23.130
Half of Dacia was annexed as a Roman province
while the other half remained free but consisted

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:25.680
of tribes that never united again.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:29.560
The land had rich gold mines which provided
a great boost to the Roman economy, not to

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:33.570
mention the tens of thousands of slaves who
were sent back to Rome.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:37.600
A few years later, Trajan’s victory in the
Dacian Wars was commemorated in a triumphal

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:41.420
monument called Trajan’s Column which still
stands today in Rome.

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:48.040
It is 115 feet tall and has a spiral bas relief
which depicts all the important events from

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:49.100
the wars.

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:53.160
It is, actually, the only source we have for
some aspects of the conflict and historians

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:57.830
still debate if it is historically accurate
or intended more as a propaganda piece.

00:13:57.830 --> 00:13:58.830
The Roman emperor also put down his version
of the story into writing.

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:02.910
Inspired by Commentarii de Bello Gallico,
Julius Caesar’s firsthand account of his

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:06.090
wars with the Gauls, Trajan wrote De bello
dacico.

00:14:06.090 --> 00:14:09.680
Unfortunately, the book is lost to history
except for just one sentence which was quoted

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:11.460
in a different work - inde Berzobim, deinde
Aizi processimus.

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:16.870
It means “We advanced to Berzobis, and then
to Aizi”.

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:24.010
Work in Rome
Shortly after his triumph, Trajan had an opportunity

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:26.590
to add the Nabataean Kingdom to his empire.

00:14:26.590 --> 00:14:29.680
This was significantly different from the
fights he had with the Dacians.

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:34.750
It was already a client-state of Rome and,
after King Rabel II Soter died in 106, all

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:38.190
it took was to move in two legions from nearby
provinces.

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:42.040
We don’t know specifics about the conquest,
but it seems that the troops encountered almost

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:43.040
no resistance.

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:47.930
Thus, the Nabataean Kingdom came to an end
as it was turned into a Roman province called

00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:49.310
Arabia Petraea.

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:53.420
The next few years of Trajan’s reign were
peaceful and they allowed the emperor to focus

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:55.640
his attention on improving things back home.

00:14:55.640 --> 00:15:01.050
He had 123 days of games and festivities to
celebrate his victory in the Dacian Wars.

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:05.750
He built and improved roads and bridges such
as the 205-mile Via Traiana which can still

00:15:05.750 --> 00:15:07.480
be partially found on the road between Benevento
and Brindisi.

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:10.880
In Rome, he put Apollodorus of Damascus’s
talents to good use.

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:15.260
The architect designed and constructed a new
market, baths and a forum which would become

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:18.680
the last of the Imperial fora built in ancient
Rome.

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:22.980
The only improvement they could have used
was a bit more creativity with the names.

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:27.600
Predictably, they were called Trajan’s Market,
the Baths of Trajan, and Trajan’s Forum.

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:32.020
Other constructions ordered by the emperor
included libraries, aqueducts, temples, and

00:15:32.020 --> 00:15:36.600
a renovation of the Circus Maximus which saw
it rebuilt entirely in stone.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:40.970
Cassius Dio mentions in passing that Trajan
was the target of several plots against him.

00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:43.480
He only names one conspirator as Crassus.

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:48.080
Obviously, these conspiracies failed and the
emperor brought Crassus before the Senate

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:49.080
to be punished.

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:53.500
What exactly happened to him and his co-conspirators
remains a mystery as the historian specifies

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:57.710
that Trajan made an oath not to shed blood
(by which he meant Roman blood, of course)

00:15:57.710 --> 00:16:00.110
and that he kept this vow.

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:08.970
The Parthian War Begins
In 113 CE, Trajan entered conflict with the

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:12.880
Parthian Empire, a powerful entity located
in ancient Iran.

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:17.240
Like Dacia, Parthia had long been a thorn
in Rome’s side as clashes between the two

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:19.350
domains went back centuries.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:23.690
Also like Dacia, Trajan sought to be the one
to solve this problem permanently and enjoy

00:16:23.690 --> 00:16:26.610
the renown and acclamations that would come
with it.

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:31.200
In fact, the emperor’s complete, true motives
remain somewhat of a hot topic among Roman

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:32.200
historians.

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:33.270
Some see them as being practical.

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:37.150
Trajan wanted to strengthen the defenses of
his eastern frontier and benefit from the

00:16:37.150 --> 00:16:40.320
economic boost of adding Parthia to the Roman
Empire.

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:42.180
Others believe it was simply in his nature.

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:46.850
Trajan was a military man, first and foremost,
and would have likely gone on warring as long

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:47.850
as he could.

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:52.420
And some opine that Trajan wanted to emulate
the conquests of Alexander the Great and secure

00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:56.310
for himself the near-divine status that the
latter enjoyed.

00:16:56.310 --> 00:17:02.470
Whatever the reason, in 113 CE, Trajan marched,
leaving Rome, never to return again.

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:05.709
Located between the two empires was the Kingdom
of Armenia.

00:17:05.709 --> 00:17:10.350
Although technically independent, it fell
under the hegemony of both Rome and Parthia

00:17:10.350 --> 00:17:15.709
and both powers had a vested interest in ensuring
that the ruler of Armenia was on their side.

00:17:15.709 --> 00:17:22.069
In 110 CE, the Parthian Prince Axidares was
installed as King of Armenia by his uncle,

00:17:22.069 --> 00:17:25.549
King Osroes I of Parthia, without Roman approval.

00:17:25.549 --> 00:17:29.320
On his way to Armenia, Trajan met an embassy
from the Parthian king.

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:34.919
In an attempt to appease Rome, Osroes had
deposed Axidares and installed his brother,

00:17:34.919 --> 00:17:39.799
Parthamasiris, as new ruler of Armenia and
sought Trajan’s approval for this change.

00:17:39.799 --> 00:17:44.299
Why exactly he thought the emperor would be
ok with one brother, but not the other, we

00:17:44.299 --> 00:17:45.299
don’t know.

00:17:45.299 --> 00:17:49.980
Trajan was gracious to his guests but mostly
ignored their requests and kept on marching.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:52.669
The conquest of Armenia was quick and bloodless.

00:17:52.669 --> 00:17:57.760
King Parthamasiris entered Trajan’s camp,
took his diadem off his head and surrendered

00:17:57.760 --> 00:17:58.760
it to the emperor.

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:02.690
He fully expected to get it back and become
a client-king of Rome in a move reminiscent

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:06.779
of what Nero did with King Tiridates half
a century prior.

00:18:06.779 --> 00:18:12.179
Instead, Trajan kept Armenia as a new Roman
province and installed a governor to rule.

00:18:12.179 --> 00:18:16.399
Parthamasiris died soon after and, although
the circumstances are not known, some have

00:18:16.399 --> 00:18:21.070
speculated he was killed on Trajan’s orders.

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:28.039
The Mesopotamian Campaign
In 115 CE, the emperor launched his Mesopotamian

00:18:28.039 --> 00:18:32.710
campaign, although the exact order of events
is unclear from ancient sources.

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:37.820
Besides the Parthian Empire, the region was
full of small kingdoms and city-states with

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:43.299
kings and satraps alike sending gifts and
envoys to ensure they obtained the favor of

00:18:43.299 --> 00:18:44.299
Rome.

00:18:44.299 --> 00:18:47.490
Trajan split his army into divisions that
went after multiple targets.

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:54.210
He was then able to capture, in quick succession,
Adiabene, Babylon, Charax, and Seleucia, culminating

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:56.809
with the conquest of the Parthian capital
of Ctesiphon.

00:18:56.809 --> 00:19:00.960
At this point, the Roman Empire reached its
greatest size in history.

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:05.519
Cassius Dio writes that Trajan was tempted
to march into India, like Alexander the Great

00:19:05.519 --> 00:19:08.080
did, but concluded that he was getting too
old for this thing.

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:12.940
Instead, he was content with making a trip
to Babylon and offering sacrifice in the house

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:14.379
where Alexander died.

00:19:14.379 --> 00:19:18.889
He did make sure to specify in writing to
the Senate, though, that he advanced further

00:19:18.889 --> 00:19:22.940
than Alexander, although keeping his new possessions
proved to be a problem.

00:19:22.940 --> 00:19:28.130
When Trajan left, many of the domains he conquered
began rebelling and the garrisons that stayed

00:19:28.130 --> 00:19:30.570
behind were not enough to maintain control.

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:34.809
It is also at this time that the emperor’s
health started to decline and some historians

00:19:34.809 --> 00:19:39.370
believe he might have suffered heat stroke
while laying siege to the city of Hatra.

00:19:39.370 --> 00:19:43.889
To make matters worse, Jewish populations
in multiple regions such as Cyrene and Egypt

00:19:43.889 --> 00:19:50.509
began uprising and Trajan had no choice but
to divert armies to deal with these revolts.

00:19:50.509 --> 00:19:58.120
Death
Trajan hoped that this would be a minor setback.

00:19:58.120 --> 00:20:02.570
He left his troops in charge of his generals
and set sail for Italy to regain his health.

00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:07.000
He then planned to return, take command again
and settle any conflicts that remained.

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:09.720
That’s not how things panned out.

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:17.090
He died suddenly on August 8, 117 CE, aged
63, in the port-town of Selinus in modern-day

00:20:17.090 --> 00:20:18.090
Turkey.

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:20.299
His cause of death remains somewhat controversial.

00:20:20.299 --> 00:20:25.360
Some sources say he died of a stroke, although
both modern and ancient historians made mention

00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:26.500
of poison.

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:28.620
His succession was also a bit contentious.

00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:34.230
His cousin Hadrian followed him on the throne,
but whether Trajan actually named him as his

00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:39.289
heir or this was arranged by his wife Plotina
after his death remains debatable.

00:20:39.289 --> 00:20:43.509
One of Hadrian’s first acts was to pull
out of Mesopotamia as it became clear that

00:20:43.509 --> 00:20:46.460
Rome did not have the resources to keep the
region.

00:20:46.460 --> 00:20:51.490
Trajan might have ultimately failed in his
Parthian campaign, but he left behind a cherished

00:20:51.490 --> 00:21:07.960
legacy that is still celebrated today.

